BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mondriaan-heritage-innovation-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T152940Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260917
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260918
SUMMARY:Deadline: Mondriaan Fund: Heritage Innovation (September 2026 round
 )
DESCRIPTION:Heritage Innovation funds projects and research tracks that con
 tribute to substantive innovation in the heritage sector. Examples given b
 y the fund include developing new narratives about cultural heritage\, str
 engthening regional collaboration across the sector\, redesigning how a co
 llection is presented\, and feasibility studies on technological applicati
 ons such as AI or gamification\, which makes this one of the more tech-adj
 acent Mondriaan schemes. WHO CAN APPLY: museums and collection-holding cul
 tural heritage institutions\, including archives\, in the Netherlands and 
 the Caribbean part of the Kingdom. Cultural organisations in the heritage 
 sector may also apply if they collaborate with one or more collection-hold
 ing institutions. All applicants must be non-profit\, not-for-profit entit
 ies. NOT FUNDED: fixed costs\, restoration\, building museums\, or regular
  operations. CONDITIONS: institutions must subscribe to the Fair Practice 
 Code\, the Culture Governance Code and the Diversity and Inclusion Code\, 
 and must pay visual artists fairly where artists are involved. AMOUNTS: pr
 ojects get up to 40% of variable project-related costs on a minimum projec
 t budget of EUR 25\,000\; research tracks get up to 70% of costs capped at
  EUR 15\,000 with no minimum budget. APPLY through the Mondriaan Fund onli
 ne system after registering an account. Deadline 17 September 2026 before 
 16:00 Dutch time / 10:00 Caribbean time.\n\nOrganisation: Mondriaan Fund\n
 Location: Museums and collection-holding heritage institutions in the Neth
 erlands and the Caribbean part of the Kingdom.\nAward: Projects: up to 40%
  of variable project-related costs\, with a minimum project budget of EUR 
 25\,000. Research tracks: up to 70% of costs\, capped at EUR 15\,000\, wit
 h no minimum budget. No application fee.\nDuration: Project or research tr
 ack. This card covers the 17 September 2026 deadline.\n\nMore: https://www
 .mondriaanfonds.nl/en/apply-for-a-grant/grants/heritage-innovation/?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.mondriaanfonds.nl/en/apply-for-a-grant/grants/heritage-inno
 vation/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Museums and collection-holding heritage institutions in the Nether
 lands and the Caribbean part of the Kingdom.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cultuurloket-digitall-implementatiebijdrage-2026@artificialnouveau.gith
 ub.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T152940Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261101
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261102
SUMMARY:Deadline: Cultuurloket DigitALL: Implementation Contribution (Imple
 mentatiebijdrage\, 2026)
DESCRIPTION:A one-off 2026 call letting Dutch cultural institutions reuse a
 nd adapt digital technology that other institutions have already built and
  published in the open DigitALL Toolkit\, for example translation software
  shared between theatres or a streaming platform adapted for a new venue. 
 Grants run up to EUR 30\,000 and cover technical modification\, integratio
 n\, production and deployment costs. Eligible applicants are museums\, the
 atres\, music venues\, festivals\, film theatres and professional performi
 ng arts producers. Applications are submitted online and assessed in order
  of receipt rather than comparatively\, with a decision within ten weeks. 
 The deadline was extended from 31 May 2026 to 1 November 2026 after high d
 emand. IMPORTANT RESTRICTION: this route cannot be used to further develop
  your own pilot internally\; it exists specifically to spread another inst
 itution's working technology across the sector. For developing something n
 ew\, use the DigitALL pilot contribution instead.\n\nOrganisation: Cultuur
 loket DigitALL\nLocation: Netherlands and the Caribbean parts of the Kingd
 om. No city or region restriction.\nAward: Up to EUR 30\,000. Covers techn
 ical modification\, system integration\, production and deployment costs. 
 Hardware purchase alone is ineligible unless clearly essential to the publ
 ic experience. No stated co-financing percentage. No application fee.\nDur
 ation: Not specified\; scoped to a single implementation.\n\nMore: https:/
 /cultuurloketdigitall.nl/implementatiebijdrage/?utm_source=artificialnouve
 augrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://cultuurloketdigitall.nl/implementatiebijdrage/?utm_source=artif
 icialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Netherlands and the Caribbean parts of the Kingdom. No city or reg
 ion restriction.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stimuleringsfonds-digital-culture-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T152940Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260819
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260820
SUMMARY:Deadline: Stimuleringsfonds: Digital Culture Grant Scheme 2026 (fin
 al round)
DESCRIPTION:The Stimuleringsfonds Digital Culture Grant Scheme (Deelregelin
 g Digitale cultuur) supports projects by makers\, researchers\, design stu
 dios and cultural institutions positioned professionally in digital cultur
 e. Solo projects can receive EUR 10\,000-25\,000\; collaborative projects 
 with partners can receive up to EUR 50\,000. At least 20 percent of total 
 project costs must be covered by co-financing. Applicants must be register
 ed with the Dutch Chamber of Commerce (KvK). The 2026 application window f
 or this round runs 12-19 August 2026 (opens 12 August 15:00\, closes 19 Au
 gust 16:00 CEST)\; in 2026 the scheme runs only two rounds and this is the
  last. No application fee. Apply via the official scheme page.\n\nOrganisa
 tion: Stimuleringsfonds Creatieve Industrie (Creative Industries Fund NL)\
 nLocation: Netherlands. Requires Dutch Chamber of Commerce (KvK) registrat
 ion.\nAward: EUR 10\,000-25\,000 for solo projects\; EUR 10\,000-50\,000 f
 or collaborative projects with partners. At least 20 percent of total proj
 ect costs must be covered by co-financing. No application fee.\nDuration: 
 Project-based.\n\nMore: https://www.stimuleringsfonds.nl/en/grants/digital
 -culture-grant-scheme?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=ref
 erral
URL:https://www.stimuleringsfonds.nl/en/grants/digital-culture-grant-scheme
 ?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Netherlands. Requires Dutch Chamber of Commerce (KvK) registration
 .
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mit-rd-samenwerking-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T152940Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: MIT R&D Collaboration Projects 2026 (MKB Innovatiestimule
 ring Topsectoren)
DESCRIPTION:The MIT R&D Collaboration Projects scheme (MIT R&D-samenwerking
 sprojecten\, part of MKB Innovatiestimulering Topsectoren) funds joint R&D
  projects by Dutch SMEs within the national Top Sectors and Mission-driven
  Innovation policy. The grant covers 35 percent of eligible costs: small p
 rojects of EUR 50\,000-200\,000 and large projects of EUR 200\,000-350\,00
 0 (with per-participant ranges of EUR 25\,000-100\,000 for small and EUR 2
 5\,000-175\,000 for large). Eligible applicants are consortia of at least 
 two collaborating SMEs\, with no single participant carrying more than 70 
 percent of costs. The national RVO round runs 9 June to 15 September 2026 
 (closes 17:00) and is a ranked tender\, not first-come-first-served\; some
  provinces run separate regional MIT windows. No application fee. Apply vi
 a RVO.\n\nOrganisation: Rijksdienst voor Ondernemend Nederland (RVO)\nLoca
 tion: Netherlands. SME consortia (minimum two collaborating SMEs).\nAward:
  Grant covering 35 percent of eligible costs. Small projects EUR 50\,000-2
 00\,000\; large projects EUR 200\,000-350\,000 per project (EUR 25\,000-10
 0\,000 or EUR 25\,000-175\,000 per participant). No application fee.\nDura
 tion: Project-based\; ranked tender (not first-come).\n\nMore: https://www
 .rvo.nl/subsidies-financiering/mit/rd-samenwerkingsprojecten?utm_source=ar
 tificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.rvo.nl/subsidies-financiering/mit/rd-samenwerkingsprojecten
 ?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Netherlands. SME consortia (minimum two collaborating SMEs).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:twente-postdoc-conversational-ai-cultural-heritage-2026@artificialnouve
 au.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T152940Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260816
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260817
SUMMARY:Deadline: University of Twente: Postdoc\, Multimodal Conversational
  AI for Cultural Heritage
DESCRIPTION:A postdoctoral position in the Human Media Interaction group at
  the University of Twente on the project 'Balancing Personalisation and Po
 lyvocality: Dialogic Interaction for Open-mindedness and Empathy in Conver
 sational AI'. The work develops and evaluates multimodal conversational ag
 ents for museum settings that hold personalisation in tension with exposur
 e to plural perspectives\, rather than optimising for engagement alone. Ta
 sks include building models that use gaze\, speech and dialogue data to in
 fer visitor engagement and openness\, designing adaptive dialogue strategi
 es\, running VR-based user studies\, and publishing in strong venues. Of t
 he current Dutch academic postings this is the closest thing to a funded c
 reative-technologist brief: museum interpretation\, multimodal sensing and
  dialogue design in one role. REQUIREMENTS: a PhD in computer science\, AI
 \, HCI or a related field\; experience with conversational AI\, dialogue s
 ystems or adaptive systems\; experience running empirical research\, prefe
 rably user studies\; strong analytical and academic writing skills\; and e
 xcellent English. Apply with a CV including publications\, a motivation le
 tter and two academic references. Contact: Shenghui Wang (shenghui.wang@ut
 wente.nl). NOTE: the AcademicTransfer listing page shows a 16 August 2026 
 deadline while the detail page states 17 August 2026\, so treat 16 August 
 as the effective cut-off.\n\nOrganisation: University of Twente\, Human Me
 dia Interaction group\nLocation: Enschede\, Netherlands\nAward: EUR 3\,546
  to 5\,538 gross per month for 40 hours per week (8 salary steps\, dependi
 ng on experience)\, plus standard Dutch university holiday and year-end al
 lowances.\nDuration: One year\, renewable for a further two years subject 
 to positive evaluation\; 40 hours per week\n\nMore: https://www.academictr
 ansfer.com/en/jobs/362707/postdoc-position-on-multimodal-conversational-ai
 -for-cultural-heritage/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=r
 eferral
URL:https://www.academictransfer.com/en/jobs/362707/postdoc-position-on-mul
 timodal-conversational-ai-for-cultural-heritage/?utm_source=artificialnouv
 eaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Enschede\, Netherlands
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:human-rights-watch-ai-strategy-solutions-lead-2026@artificialnouveau.gi
 thub.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T152940Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: Human Rights Watch: AI Strategy and Solutions Lead (one-y
 ear fixed term)
DESCRIPTION:Human Rights Watch is hiring an AI Strategy and Solutions Lead 
 into its Operations Department to build the foundation for responsible AI 
 adoption across the organisation. The role deliberately sits between strat
 egy and execution: assessing organisational readiness\, mapping partnershi
 ps and governance\, and also prototyping and shipping working systems. THE
  WORK\, in four strands. (1) STRATEGY AND READINESS: with the CIO and the 
 Information Security and Technology (IST) leadership team\, build an AI st
 rategy covering data foundation requirements\, infrastructure gaps and org
 anisational readiness\; assess current state and shape a roadmap balancing
  quick wins against long-term capacity\; research what peer nonprofit\, hu
 manitarian and human rights organisations are doing and find collaboration
  openings. (2) FLAGSHIP SOLUTIONS: work with departments to find high-impa
 ct use cases\, translate business needs into technical approaches\, build 
 and deploy AI/ML solutions with measurable value (time saved\, efficiency\
 , better outcomes)\, using the Microsoft AI ecosystem (Copilot Studio\, Az
 ure AI\, Power Platform) among other tools\, and oversee consultants and v
 endors to extend delivery capacity. (3) ADOPTION AND CAPACITY: balance onb
 oarding staff onto AI tools against building custom solutions where they a
 mplify the organisation's strengths\; write training material pitched at d
 ifferent work areas and skill levels covering prompting and responsible us
 e\; support working groups and communities of practice\; produce playbooks
  and guides for non-technical staff. (4) GOVERNANCE: partner with IST lead
 ership on risk assessment frameworks\, align solutions with security\, inf
 rastructure and architecture requirements\, contribute to AI policy develo
 pment and its operationalisation\, set standards for evaluating proofs of 
 concept and MVPs\, and engage business units so outcomes are co-owned\; al
 l of it balanced against data quality\, security and resource constraints 
 and HRW's mission as a human rights organisation. WHO THEY WANT: a bachelo
 r's degree or equivalent work experience in computer science\, data scienc
 e\, information systems\, mathematics\, AI/ML implementation or a related 
 technical field\; a minimum of seven years' relevant experience including 
 two to three years on AI and two to three years implementing AI/ML in prod
 uction\; experience contributing to technology strategy and assessing orga
 nisational readiness for new technology\; strong Python and ML library/fra
 mework skills\; hands-on predictive modelling\, data science and statistic
 al analysis\; experience with low-code/no-code AI platforms such as Copilo
 t Studio or Power Platform\; a track record of scoping and delivering unde
 r resource constraints\; a grasp of both generative AI and traditional ML\
 ; and the ability to explain technical concepts plainly to mixed audiences
 \, engage stakeholders and lead technology adoption as an internal partner
  to business teams. PREFERRED: nonprofit\, humanitarian or human rights ex
 perience\; data privacy\, security and ethical AI knowledge\; familiarity 
 with responsible AI frameworks and governance models\; vendor and consulta
 nt management\; and a background in politically sensitive or high-risk env
 ironments. APPLY: through the HRW job portal with a cover letter and CV\, 
 preferably as PDFs\, by 30 August 2026. Only complete applications are rev
 iewed and only shortlisted candidates are contacted\; no calls or email en
 quiries. HRW states that it does not discriminate on the basis of disabili
 ty\, age\, gender identity and expression\, national origin\, race and eth
 nicity\, religious beliefs\, sexual orientation or criminal record.\n\nOrg
 anisation: Human Rights Watch (HRW)\, Operations Department\nLocation: Mus
 t be within commuting distance of an HRW office: Amsterdam\, Amman\, Berli
 n\, Brussels\, London\, Stockholm\, New York\, Washington DC\, Miami\, Sao
  Paulo\, Toronto or Chicago. Reports to the Chief Information Officer in B
 erlin. No relocation support and no work-authorisation sponsorship.\nAward
 : USD 102\,000 to 112\,000 plus employer-paid benefits if based in the US\
 ; ranges outside the US are set by local market conditions. No application
  fee.\nDuration: One year fixed-term\, full time. Application deadline 30 
 August 2026.\n\nMore: https://job-boards.greenhouse.io/humanrightswatch/jo
 bs/8350654002?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://job-boards.greenhouse.io/humanrightswatch/jobs/8350654002?utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Must be within commuting distance of an HRW office: Amsterdam\, Am
 man\, Berlin\, Brussels\, London\, Stockholm\, New York\, Washington DC\, 
 Miami\, Sao Paulo\, Toronto or Chicago. Reports to the Chief Information O
 fficer in Berlin. No relocation support and no work-authorisation sponsors
 hip.
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
